# Comments

The default number of comments in code you write is **zero**. Well-named code
explains itself. Add a comment only when you can name a concrete reason a future
reader would be confused **without** it.

## The one test

Before writing any comment, ask: _"Does this explain something the code cannot?"_

- If the comment restates, summarizes, or paraphrases the code below it → delete it.
- If it explains **why** (a non-obvious reason, trade-off, caveat, workaround, or
  library/platform quirk) → keep it.

If you cannot articulate the "why", the code needs a better name, not a comment.

## Never write these

- Narration of what the next line/block does (`// loop over users`, `// return result`).
- Restating a name in prose (`// the user id` above `const userId`).
- Change logs or intent about your edit (`// added retry`, `// now handles null`,
  `// updated to use X`). Git history is the changelog, not the source.
- Section banners, decorative dividers, or `// ---- helpers ----`.
- Commented-out code. Delete it.
- Redundant JSDoc that only repeats the signature and param names.

## Good comments explain why

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — narrates the obvious / logs the change
// Increment the counter
counter += 1
// Loop over entries and filter logs
entries.filter((entry) => entry.endsWith('.log'))
// Added catch so the app doesn't crash
try { risky() } catch {}

// ✅ GOOD — explains a reason the code cannot
// Rollovers within the same millisecond would collide on the timestamp.
while (fs.existsSync(candidate)) { ... }

// Don't cache a rejected promise: clear it so the next call can retry
// instead of reusing the failed attempt.
sessionPromise ??= createSession().catch(() => { sessionPromise = undefined; throw error })
```

## Before finishing

Re-read every comment you added or touched and delete any that fail the one test
above. Do not leave a comment just because the surrounding code already had that
style. When suppressing a linter rule, justify it inline
(`// eslint-disable-next-line ... -- reason`).
